0051 — a bundle names its modules by what they are, not where they are.

Bun writes each module's name on the line above its code when it does not
minify whitespace, and the name is the module's realpath relative to the
build process's working directory — not to `root`, which main sets to the
same directory (measured on Bun 1.3.11 by building one entry from two cwds
with one `root`). So the bytes of a bundle depend on three locations that
are not inputs to it: where the app sits, where the PocketJS tree sits, and
where the build was started. Built from a staging directory with a random
name, the app's modules were `// dist/captions-job-Xq3/main.tsx`; built
from a project outside the tree, every framework module was `//
../../../Users/<someone>/<checkout>/vendor/pocketjs/framework/src/index.ts`;
built against an installed package, `// node_modules/<pkg>/vendor/pocketjs/
framework/src/index.ts`. Nothing the bundle executes reads a name, and a
host that hashes the bundle to say which inputs produced a picture reads
every one.

`framework/compiler/bundle-names.ts` names a module by what it is:

  inside the PocketJS tree        framework/src/index.ts   relative to the tree
  inside the app's directory      ./app.tsx                relative to the app
  the build's system-layer entry  .entries/main.ts         relative to --outdir
  inside a node_modules           node_modules/x/index.js  from the last node_modules/
  anywhere else                   ../shared/theme.ts       relative to the app

The tree comes first, so an app inside it keeps the name a build from the
tree's root gives it today (`apps/hero/app.tsx`), and a build of main's own
apps from its root writes the bytes it did. A namespaced module keeps its
namespace and has an absolute path after it named by the same rules, so
main's `pocketjs-generated-styles:/<the checkout>/…` would come out
`pocketjs-generated-styles:framework/src/styles.generated.ts`.

`tools/build.ts` rewrites the name lines after pass 2 and writes the bundle
over Bun's. The lines to rewrite are read off the metafile main already
asks for: an input's key is the name Bun printed, and `bytesInOutput` says
whether the module put code in the bundle. Bun prints a name line above
each run of a module's code, and a module can have two — an entry that
exports has its namespace object (`var exports_main`) placed ahead of the
modules it imports, under a second copy of its name — so every whole line
equal to `// <key>` is rewritten. The only other text a bundle can carry as
such a line is the inside of a template literal, so the rewrite is checked:
Bun's transpiler, which drops comments, must print the same program before
and after (11 ms for both passes over the reel's 500 KB bundle). If it does
not, the build fails naming the line, rather than shipping a rewritten
string. A module that put code in the bundle with no name line fails it
too, because Bun then printed names some other way than this reads them.

pocket-motion `tests/build-location.test.ts` builds one composition from
its checkout, from a randomly named directory under `dist/` and from a
project outside the repository reached through a symlink, and holds the
three bundles byte-identical. Red without this patch: the staged bundle
differs in the app's one name line and the symlinked one in all 46 of
the framework's. `tests/bundle-names.test.ts` there holds the rules and
both refusals, and is red on each with its check removed; its
`tests/style-table.test.ts` builds an entry that exports, which a first
form of this patch, requiring one name line per module, refused. Every
bundle of the pocket-motion digest ledger changes bytes in its name lines
and in nothing else; every frame digest is unchanged, and each of its 42
compositions rebuilt from a randomly named directory is the same bundle.

`git apply` skips everything above the first `diff --git`.

